# GLM-5.3-Flash EXL3 — EXPERIMENTAL 3× DGX Spark (TP=3)
# Copy to .env.tp3 and edit. ./start-tp3.sh does this on first run.
#
# Sourced AFTER .env, so these values win over the 2× TP=2 knobs
# (TP=2, NNODES=2, DFLASH_DRAFT_TP=2, NCCL_CROSS_NIC=0). start.sh never
# reads this file.

# --- ranks (edit these) ------------------------------------------------
HEAD_IP=10.0.0.1
WORKER_IP=10.0.0.2
WORKER2_IP=10.0.0.3
# Mixed OS accounts (unset = same user as the head / WORKER_USER):
# WORKER_USER=
# WORKER2_USER=
# WORKER2_HOME=/home/you
# WORKER2_SSH=you@10.0.0.3

# --- fabric --------------------------------------------------------------
# Three Sparks need a RoCE path among all ranks (QSFP triangle on both CX7
# ports, or a switch). NCCL cannot use the 10.0.0.x loopback aliases.
# Per-rank pins are what the containers get.
#
# Wire the triangle as a DIRECTED RING: each node's Port0 (the cage next to
# the RJ45) to the NEXT node's Port1 — head f0 -> worker2 f1, worker2 f0 ->
# worker1 f1, worker1 f0 -> head f1. NCCL pairs NIC index to NIC index per
# channel, and NCCL_IB_SUBNET_AWARE_ROUTING can only fix a pair when one side
# already points at the right peer; a mirrored ring leaves one pair that can
# never connect (ibv_modify_qp ... Connection timed out). Reordering
# NCCL_IB_HCA does not help — NCCL enumerates devices in system order.
# One /24 per cable, MTU 9000, ipv4.never-default yes.
#
# Dual-port ring example (comma-separated HCAs / IFs):
#   HEAD_CX7_IB=rocep1s0f0,rocep1s0f1
#   HEAD_CX7_IF=enp1s0f0np0,enp1s0f1np1
NCCL_CROSS_NIC=1
HEAD_CX7_IF=enp1s0f1np1
HEAD_CX7_IB=rocep1s0f1
WORKER_CX7_IF=enp1s0f0np0
WORKER_CX7_IB=rocep1s0f0
WORKER2_CX7_IF=enp1s0f0np0
WORKER2_CX7_IB=rocep1s0f0
# Per-rank RoCEv2 GID (empty = NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX). Disable IPv6 on the fabric
# profiles or the index drifts. Inspect:
#   cat /sys/class/infiniband/<dev>/ports/1/gids/{0..7}
# HEAD_GID=3
# WORKER_GID=3
# WORKER2_GID=3
NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=3
NCCL_DEBUG=WARN

# --- serve (TP=3) --------------------------------------------------------
TP=3
NNODES=3
# The DFlash2 drafter has 32 attention heads and 8 KV heads — neither divides
# by 3, so it cannot be sharded across the ranks. 1 = rank 0 only.
DFLASH_DRAFT_TP=1
PORT=8888
MASTER_PORT=29521
CONTAINER_HEAD=glm53-exl3-tp3-head
CONTAINER_WORKER=glm53-exl3-tp3-w1
CONTAINER_WORKER2=glm53-exl3-tp3-w2

# --- TP=3 shape fixes ----------------------------------------------------
# GLM-5.3-Flash has 64 attention heads, 64 KV heads and moe_intermediate_size
# 2048. None divides by 3, so three overrides are what make TP=3 load at all.
#
# Pad the head counts to 66 (3 x 22) via --hf-overrides. Empty = leave the
# checkpoint alone, which is right only for a model that already divides by 3.
TP3_HEAD_OVERRIDE=66
# Give each rank whole experts (288 routed / 3 = 96) instead of slicing
# moe_intermediate_size by 3. Required at TP=3.
ENABLE_EXPERT_PARALLEL=1
# Run the vision tower data-parallel across ranks, same divisibility reason.
# Empty = vLLM's default ("weights").
MM_ENCODER_TP_MODE=data

# --- weights: ranks read the head's HF cache over NFSv4 -------------------
# The checkpoint is ~164 GiB. start.sh rsyncs a full copy to every worker; at
# three nodes that is ~330 GiB of duplicate disk plus two long first-boot
# copies. Instead the head exports its HF cache over NFSv4 on ConnectX and each
# rank mounts it read-only as a docker volume — same pattern as DS4.1
# (files/nfs-share.sh) and the live vllm-fn-nfs exporter on this kit.
# Safe read-only: the serve container runs HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 and its writable
# Triton/TileLang/vLLM caches are separate node-local mounts.
#
# LEAVE THIS COMMENTED to inherit whatever .env says. This file is sourced
# AFTER .env, so an uncommented NFS_SHARE here silently overrides the .env
# value — set it here only to deliberately differ from the 2-node default.
# At three nodes this is the difference between one 164 GiB copy and three.
# NFS_SHARE=1
NFS_VOLUME=glm53-weights
# An exporter already serving this cache (vllm-fn-nfs / glm53fp8-nfs /
# dsv41-nfs) is reused and its client list merged; otherwise files/nfs-server
# is built and run as NFS_CONTAINER.
NFS_CONTAINER=glm53-nfs
NFS_IMAGE=glm53-nfs:local
# Who may mount. Include every rank's fabric subnet, not just 10.0.0.x.
NFS_CLIENTS=10.0.0.2,10.0.0.3,10.0.22.0/24,10.0.23.0/24,10.0.33.0/24
# Which head address each rank mounts. MUST be the ConnectX address on that
# rank's cable — 10.0.0.x are loopback aliases and the launcher refuses them,
# because mounting over the management link turns a 164 GiB load into an
# overnight job. Unset = autodetect via `ip route get` toward the rank's fabric
# IP (WORKER_FABRIC_IP / WORKER2_FABRIC_IP), which is right for a direct ring.
# NFS_SERVER_IP_1=10.0.22.1
# NFS_SERVER_IP_2=10.0.23.1
# WORKER_FABRIC_IP=10.0.22.2
# WORKER2_FABRIC_IP=10.0.23.3
# Client mount options. nconnect=8 is what makes a cold load use the cable.
# NFS_OPTS_CLIENT=nfsvers=4.2,ro,nconnect=8,rsize=1048576,wsize=1048576,hard,timeo=600

# --- memory --------------------------------------------------------------
# Three nodes hold the same weights in more total memory, so the KV pool and
# context can grow. Reference points from the 3x recipes this borrows from:
# util 0.83 left only 4-6 GB host free per node, 0.78 left 9-12 GB. This kit's
# UMA has no headroom to spare — see the OOM notes in README — so start low
# and raise it only with MemAvailable in front of you.
GPU_MEM_UTIL=0.80
MAX_MODEL_LEN=1000000
# Uncomment to pin the KV pool instead of letting it take the whole budget:
# EXTRA_ARGS="--kv-cache-memory-bytes 15032385536"
